<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="dot-product-complete">
    <title>Dot Product (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We've seen natural ways to combine vectors. There are two ways we can make sense of 
                        <q>vector multiplication</q>:
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>\vvec \cdot \wvec =</m> scalar (which is what we'll discuss here)
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>\vvec \times \wvec =</m> vector (next time)
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Two Perspectives</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                We can compute a dot product in two different ways:
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    If <m>\vvec =\langle x_1 ,y_1 \rangle</m> and <m>\wvec =\langle x_2 ,y_2 \rangle</m>,
                                    then
                                    <md>
                                        \vvec \cdot \wvec = x_1x_2 + y_1y_2
                                    </md>.
                                    
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>\vvec \cdot \wvec = |\vvec| |\wvec| \cos(\theta)</m>, where <m>0 \leq \theta \leq \pi</m> is 
                                    the (smallest) angle between the vectors.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Some general remarks regarding these perspectives:
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    From the second perspective, we can think of <m>\vvec \cdot \wvec</m> as a measure of how close to parallel the two vectors are.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    The dot product is commutative (taking the product in either order produces the same scalar).
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>\vvec \cdot \vvec = |\vvec|^2</m>, or <m>|\vvec| = \sqrt{\vvec \cdot \vvec}</m>. 
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise>
                <title>Examples: Computations </title>
             
                <task workspace="3in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>\vvec = \langle -3,1 \rangle</m> and <m>\wvec = \langle 1,2 \rangle</m>. Find <m>\theta</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Use the first perspective, we can compute <m>\vvec \cdot \wvec = -1</m> (so we know <m>\theta \gt \pi/2</m>). 
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Using the second perspective, we can write <m>\cos(\theta) = -1/\sqrt{50}</m>. A good first attempt would be to
                            solve <m>\theta = \arccos(-1/\sqrt{50})</m>, which after recalling the range of <m>\arccos</m> is 
                            <m>[0,\pi]</m>, we realize does give the correct angle (which is approximately <m>1.713</m> radians).
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>

                <task workspace="3in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                          Find a unit vector perpendicular to <m>\vvec = \langle 1,5 \rangle</m>. 
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>\uvec= \langle x, y\rangle</m>. Then <m>\vvec \cdot \uvec = 0</m> implies <m>x+5y =0</m>. 
                            Since <m>\uvec</m> is a unit vector, <m>\uvec \cdot \uvec =1</m>, which implies <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>.
                            Solving this system of equations gives two solutions, <m>\uvec_1 = \langle -5 \sqrt{1/26}, \sqrt{1/26} \rangle </m>
                            and <m>\uvec_2 = \langle 5 \sqrt{1/26}, -\sqrt{1/26} \rangle </m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
          
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="8in">
        <title>Vector Projections</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
             What is the component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> pointing in the direction of <m>\mathbf{D}</m>?
            </p>
           
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We call this the projection (or shadow) of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> onto <m>\mathbf{D}</m>, written
                <m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf{D}} \mathbf{F}</m>. 
            </p>
            <p>
                For any two non-zero vectors <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\wvec</m>, we can project <m>\vvec</m> onto <m>\wvec</m>
                using the definition
                <md>
                \text{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{\vvec \cdot \wvec}{ \wvec \cdot \wvec} \cdot \wvec
                    
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </solution>
        
            
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>

        <exercise>
        <title>Examples: Projections</title>
        
         <task workspace="3in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                         Project <m>\wvec = \langle 4,1 \rangle</m> onto <m>\vvec = \langle 3,5 \rangle</m>. 
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <m>\text{proj}_{\vvec} \wvec = \langle 1.5, 2.5 \rangle</m>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                        See the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/h9ijrhiqlf">Desmos interactive for Vector basics</url>
                        for a visual
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>

                <task workspace="3in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                         Project <m>\wvec = \langle -2,1 \rangle</m> onto <m>\vvec = \langle 2,-2 \rangle</m>. 
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <m>\text{proj}_{\wvec} \vvec = \langle -1.5, 1.5 \rangle</m>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                        See the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/h9ijrhiqlf">Desmos interactive for Vector basics</url>
                        for a visual
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
            
        </exercise>
    
    </page>


       
    </worksheet>
